  "prompt_text": "The figure explains how beach width and storm-driven groundwater changes affect freshwater on a sandy coast. Overall, it shows that adding sand to widen or raise the beach can enlarge the underground freshwater reservoir, but storms can temporarily push groundwater upward and cause flooding.\n\nIn the upper schematic, beach nourishment creates a broader dune and beach area. Rainfall infiltrates this larger land surface, increasing the volume and depth of fresh groundwater stored above deeper saline groundwater. The post-nourishment freshwater lens is shown as thicker or deeper than before nourishment, suggesting improved freshwater resources inland near homes.\n\nThe middle and lower schematics focus on storm conditions. During elevated sea levels and wave attack, seawater appears to infiltrate the beach face, raising the groundwater table inland. A sequence of dashed lines indicates the groundwater mound shifting through time from a pre-storm level to a storm peak. In the final panel, if this raised groundwater surface intersects the land surface, groundwater flows out of the beach and low-lying back-beach area as exfiltration, producing localized flooding near infrastructure. The main message is that wider beaches can enhance freshwater storage, but storms can also drive short-term groundwater flooding hazards.",
